 Creating Adobe JavaScript to force a text field to display columns can be achieved by using the `textFlow` and `Paragraph` objects. Here's a basic example of how you can structure your code to display multiple columns in a text field:

```javascript
// Assuming you have a text field with the id "myTextfield"
var textField = app.documents[0].textFrames.item("myTextfield");

// Create a new text flow
var textFlow = new TextFlow();

// Add your text to the text flow
textFlow.addText("Column 1 content\nColumn 2 content\nColumn 3 content");

// Create a paragraph for each column
var column1 = new Paragraph();
var column2 = new Paragraph();
var column3 = new Paragraph();

// Set the width of each column
column1.width = textField.width / 3;
column2.width = textField.width / 3;
column3.width = textField.width / 3;

// Add the columns to the text flow
textFlow.add(column1);
textFlow.add(column2);
textFlow.add(column3);

// Apply the text flow to the text field
textField.textFlow = textFlow;
```

This script creates a text flow with multiple columns and applies it to a text field. You can adjust the number of columns, their width, and the content of each column according to your needs. Keep in mind that this is a simple example, and you may need to adapt the code to fit your specific use case.

Hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions or need further assistance.

